[A-13] What is the planned sample size and statistical power?
-----------------------------------------------------------------
{
    "result": {
        "response": "## Planned Sample Size and Statistical Power\n\n### Sample Size\n- A total of **90 patients** will be enrolled competitively across approximately **25\u201330 investigational sites globally**, accounting for an expected **~20% dropout rate** (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 29; p. 12).\n- The core randomized sample is **N=72 patients (n=24 per arm)**, equally randomized into **three arms**: TJ301 300 mg Q2W, TJ301 600 mg Q2W, and placebo (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 29; p. 66).\n\n### Statistical Power\nUsing **Pearson's chi-square test without continuity correction**, the trial is powered as follows:\n\n1. **83% power** to detect a trend (p < 0.05, one-sided) if the true remission rate difference at **Week 12** between the placebo and the **highest dose group (600 mg Q2W)** is **30%** (10% for placebo vs. 40% for 600 mg Q2W) (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 12; p. 66).\n\n2. **70% power** to reach a statistically significant result (p < 0.05, one-sided) if the remission rate difference between placebo and treatment **(300 mg Q2W and 600 mg Q2W combined)** is **20%** (10% vs. 30%) (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 12; p. 66).\n\n### Nature of the Trial\nThis is a **proof-of-concept (exploratory) trial**, not aimed at confirming primary efficacy, but rather at exploring preliminary indications of efficacy and safety to inform a decision about proceeding into full development. Statistical testing will be performed at both the **one-sided 20%** and **one-sided 5%** significance levels (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 66; p. 67).",
        "sources": [
            {
                "name": "Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf",
                "page": 12,
                "section": "Sample Size",
                "exactText": "A sample size of N=72 (n=24 per treatment/placebo arm) patients is expected to achieve a power of 83 % of detecting a trend (p< 0.05, one-sided) if the true remission rate difference (at Week 12) between the placebo and highest dose groups (600 mg Q2W) is 30% (10% for the placebo and 40% for the highest dose) using Pearson's chi-square test without continuity correction. The trial also has 70 % power to reach a statistically significant result (p<0.05, one-sided) in case the remission rate difference between placebo and treatment (300 mg Q2W and 600 mg Q2W combined together) is 20% (10% vs 30%). Considering the dropout rate of approximately 20 %, a total of 90 patients will be enrolled competitively.",
