    "result": {
        "response": "## CRP Use in Randomisation Stratification\n\nIn the **current version (Version 1.1)** of the protocol, **CRP is no longer used as a stratification factor** for randomisation. It was replaced by other criteria.\n\n### Historical Context (Version 1.0 \u2192 Version 1.1 Amendment)\n\nIn **Version 1.0**, elevated CRP levels (\u22653 mg/L) *were* one of the stratification factors:\n> Patients were stratified by **elevated (\u22653 mg/L) CRP levels** and stable conventional treatment (prior treatment with amino salicylates, corticosteroids, and immunosuppressive drugs). (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 92)\n\nHowever, this was **changed in Version 1.1**, where the stratification factors were updated to:\n1. **Prior corticosteroids treatment (yes/no)**\n2. **Consent to participate in PK substudy (yes/no)**\n\n(Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 92; p. 36)\n\n### Current Role of CRP in the Study\n\nAlthough CRP is no longer a stratification factor, it is still used in the study in the following ways:\n- As an **exploratory biomarker**, measured from blood, tracking *change from Baseline to Weeks 4, 8, and 12*. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 25)\n- Analysed at **local laboratory sites** at specified visits. (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 45)\n\nIn summary, CRP was a stratification factor only under **Version 1.0** of the protocol and was removed in the amendment to **Version 1.1**.",
