[D-34] What is the molecular mechanism of action of TJ301?
-----------------------------------------------------------------
{
    "result": {
        "response": "## Molecular Mechanism of Action of TJ301 (Olamkicept)\n\nTJ301 (FE 999301, Olamkicept) is a **first-in-class, selective IL-6 trans-signalling inhibitor** that works through the following molecular mechanism:\n\n### Structural Basis\nTJ301 is a selective **IL-6/sIL-6R complex trap** consisting of:\n- **Two complete extracellular domains of gp130** (the common signal transducer of IL-6-type cytokines)\n- **Dimerised by fusion to the fragment crystallisable (Fc) region of human IgG1** (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 22\u201323)\n\n### Target and Selectivity\nTJ301 **targets and neutralises the IL-6/sIL-6R complex**, thereby inhibiting the **trans-signalling pathway** specifically. Critically, it does so **without any interaction with either IL-6 or IL-6R individually**, distinguishing it from other anti-IL-6 or anti-IL-6R products that block both IL-6 signalling pathways (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 23).\n\n### Disease Context and Downstream Effects\nIn IBD patients (Crohn's disease and UC):\n- **Elevated IL-6 and sIL-6R** lead to increased formation of the IL-6/sIL-6R complex in serum (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 22)\n- Mucosal T-cells show strong evidence for **IL-6 trans-signalling with activation of STAT-3**, and the anti-apoptotic factors **bcl-2 and bcl-xl** (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 31)\n- By blocking this complex, TJ301 is anticipated to **induce apoptosis of disease-perpetuating T-cells**, which are assumed to drive long-lasting secretion of matrix-degrading substances such as **chemokines and matrix metalloproteinases** that promote transmural inflammation (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 31)\n\n### Expected Clinical Advantage\nTJ301 is expected to be **as effective as existing biologics but safer and more suited for early and long-term use**, owing to its selective inhibition of trans-signalling only (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 23).",
        "sources": [
            {
                "name": "Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf",
                "page": 23,
                "section": "1.1 Background",
                "exactText": "TJ301 targets and neutralises the IL-6/sIL-6R complex thereby inhibiting the trans-signalling pathway, without any interaction with either IL-6 or IL-6R individually, which is different from other anti-IL6 or anti-IL6R products in development to block IL-6 two signaling pathways. TJ301 is expected to be as effective as existing biologics but safer and more suited for early and long-term use.",
