    "result": {
        "response": "Based on the provided context, corticosteroids are mentioned in two key areas:\n\n---\n\n## Corticosteroids in the TJ301 UC Protocol\n\n### 1. Clinical Background & Side Effects (Benefit/Risk Section)\nOral, systemic corticosteroids are used for **induction of remission** in UC patients not responding to 5-ASA. However, they carry **frequent and occasionally serious side effects**, including:\n- Hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA)-axis suppression\n- Hyperglycaemia/insulin resistance\n- Cataracts\n- Osteoporosis *(notably, IBD itself is a risk factor for osteopenia/osteoporosis)*\n\nBecause of these risks, **systemic corticosteroids are considered undesirable for maintenance treatment** (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 23).\n\nAdditionally, since corticosteroids (along with thiopurines) are **not effective in all patients** \u2014 particularly in the moderately to severely active IBD population \u2014 there is a recognized need for novel second- and third-line treatment options like TJ301 (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 23).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Concomitant Therapy Restrictions During the Trial\nPatients on stable-dose concomitant corticosteroid treatment at **Visit 2** are subject to a specific exception:\n> *\"Tapering of corticosteroids is allowed at the discretion of the Investigator\"*\n\nAll other concomitant UC treatments must remain at a **stable dose** throughout the trial (Protocol_Ulcerative Colitis.pdf, p. 37).",
